<main><article><h1>Two product pages in under an hour</h1>
<p>The maintainer of lfm runs an autonomous coding agent that builds and sells small digital products. On 23
October 2026 it used lfm 0.1.7 for two sales pages, from <code>lfm init</code> to live. This page shows what
lfm checked and what it caught, with numbers from the build logs and from the live pages.</p>
<dl class="figures">
<div><dt>First lfm init to both pages live</dt><dd>44 minutes</dd></div>
<div><dt>Both release compiles</dt><dd>0 errors, 0 warnings</dd></div>
<div><dt>Lighthouse mobile, both pages</dt><dd>100 / 100 / 100 / 100</dd></div>
</dl>
<div class="live-pages">
<figure><img src="assets/images/sans-slop-live-640.webp" srcset="assets/images/sans-slop-live-400.webp 400w, assets/images/sans-slop-live-640.webp 640w, assets/images/sans-slop-live-1280.webp 1280w" sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 476px, calc(100vw - 64px)" width="1280" height="800" alt="Sans Slop live page: a copy editor’s proof with marked-up text and a red purchase button."><figcaption>Sans Slop. Live page, captured 23 October 2026.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><img src="assets/images/grok-bot-templates-live-640.webp" srcset="assets/images/grok-bot-templates-live-400.webp 400w, assets/images/grok-bot-templates-live-640.webp 640w, assets/images/grok-bot-templates-live-1280.webp 1280w" sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 476px, calc(100vw - 64px)" width="1280" height="800" loading="lazy" alt="Grok Bot Squad Kit live page: a dispatch room layout with bot handoffs and a purchase offer."><figcaption>Grok Bot Squad Kit. Live page, captured 23 October 2026.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<h2>What it shipped</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="{{.urls.sansSlop}}">Sans Slop</a> (€29 launch price, €39 from 1 December 2026). A pack that
steers coding agents away from UI and copy that reads as generated: project rules for Cursor, Claude Code and
Codex, worked before/after examples and a local checker that runs after a draft. Its word and phrase lists are
extracted from lfm&#x27;s own copy and design checks, under lfm&#x27;s MIT license. The pack has 43 files, 526
hard-list entries plus 54 watch words, and a 763-byte always-on core file. Its build runs 23 tests.</li>
<li><a href="{{.urls.squadKit}}">Grok Bot Squad Kit</a> (€59 download, €249 with a written custom plan).
Templates for running several Grok Bot bots with clear handoffs and approval stops: 12 bot blueprints, 16
routine specs and 8 operating guides, plus an eight-question ban-risk check that runs in the browser.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Timeline</h2>
<p>23 October 2026, CEST; from the git log and lfm’s compile logs.</p>
<ol class="timeline">
<li><span class="time">22:44</span><div><code>lfm init</code> for Sans Slop</div></li>
<li><span class="time">22:45</span><div><code>lfm init</code> for the Squad Kit</div></li>
<li><span class="time">22:58</span><div>first Sans Slop release compile: 4 warnings</div></li>
<li><span class="time">23:04</span><div>first Squad Kit release compile: 4 warnings</div></li>
<li><span class="time">23:05</span><div>Squad Kit release compile: 0 errors, 0 warnings</div></li>
<li><span class="time">23:13</span><div>Squad Kit live (commit time)</div></li>
<li><span class="time">23:21</span><div>Sans Slop release compile: 0 errors, 0 warnings</div></li>
<li><span class="time">about 23:25</span><div>Sans Slop live; 23:28 the commit that records it</div></li>
</ol>
<p>44 minutes from the first <code>lfm init</code> to the commit that recorded both pages live. The agent
handed the long writing and coding jobs to Codex CLI and reviewed the results; every page change went through
<code>lfm compile</code> again.</p>
<h2>What lfm did</h2>
<p>Research. lfm&#x27;s research skill has the agent collect dated buyer posts and competing pages first; the
<code>research.balance</code> check holds them to minimums (60 posts, 5 platforms, 12 competing pages) and
every compile report counts them. Each page&#x27;s research ended at 70 dated buyer posts on 5 platforms and
12 competing pages. For Sans Slop the posts came mostly from Hacker News (40%), Reddit (40%) and GitHub (14%);
for the Squad Kit from Reddit (40%), X (40%) and the Cursor Forum (17%). The concepts built on them,
&quot;Copy editor&#x27;s proof&quot; and &quot;Dispatch room&quot;, cite 37 and 30 of those posts.</p>
<h3>Checks that caught real problems</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>commerce.checkout</code> (Sans Slop): the buy button still pointed at a placeholder payment link.
lfm flagged lfm.json line 26 on every compile until the real link went in.</li>
<li><code>copy.machine</code> (Sans Slop, first compile): &quot;AI-text detectors would likely flag the
page&#x27;s copy as generated (262 words) … 88% of paragraphs have two to four sentences&quot;. The copy was
rewritten; the next compile at 22:59 was clean of it.</li>
<li><code>release.layout</code> (Squad Kit): the release build rendered the page in Chrome and found that the
heading font Chivo had no glyph for the right and down arrows, and the mono font had none for the box-drawing corner, horizontal line and Roman numeral two, so a fallback font drew
them. The agent set the arrows in a face that has them, replaced the box-drawing line with a CSS border and
wrote II.</li>
<li><code>lighthouse.network-dependency-tree-insight</code> (Squad Kit): a second font loaded late in a
request chain; the fix was a preload. Both fixes landed in one commit and the next release compile had 0
warnings.</li>
<li>Other Sans Slop builds that evening also flagged a home link whose accessible name did not match its
visible text (<code>lighthouse.label-content-name-mismatch</code>) and text smaller than 12px on about half
the page (<code>lighthouse.font-size</code>: 51.56% legible).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Release review</h3>
<p>Each release compile rendered the page in Chrome and saved screenshots at phone and desktop widths (55 for
Sans Slop, 65 for the Squad Kit). The Sans Slop frames were reviewed at 390, 1280 and 1440 pixels wide before
deploying.</p>
<h2>Results, measured live</h2>
<p>Measured from our build machine on 23 October 2026 at 23:31 CEST: 5 curl requests per page for server
response, one Lighthouse 12.8.2 run per page with the mobile and the desktop preset. One run from one machine;
your numbers will differ.</p>
<div class="measurements" role="region" aria-label="Live measurements" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Live page measurements; Lighthouse scores are performance, accessibility, best practices and SEO.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Measurement</th><th scope="col">Sans Slop</th><th scope="col">Grok Bot Squad Kit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">HTML, as sent (brotli)</th><td>8.6 KB</td><td>11.5 KB</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">HTML, uncompressed</th><td>24.9 KB</td><td>35.6 KB</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Everything the first view loads</th><td>64.5 KB in 6 requests</td><td>60.4 KB in 6 requests</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Time to first byte, median of 5</th><td>0.20 s</td><td>0.19 s</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lighthouse mobile</th><td>100 / 100 / 100 / 100</td><td>100 / 100 / 100 / 100</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Largest Contentful Paint, mobile</th><td>1.3 s</td><td>1.2 s</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Largest Contentful Paint, desktop</th><td>0.3 s</td><td>0.3 s</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Total Blocking Time, mobile</th><td>0 ms</td><td>80 ms</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Cumulative Layout Shift, mobile</th><td>0.006</td><td>0.001</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The first-view totals include a 1 KB script the CDN injects into both pages. Both pages compile today with
lfm 0.1.7 at 0 errors and 0 warnings.</p>
<h2>What we&#x27;d improve in lfm</h2>
<ul>
<li>The Squad Kit page was first built inside a sandbox that blocked local ports. There <code>lfm compile
--release</code> crashed with a Go panic (&quot;httptest: failed to listen on a port&quot;) instead of
reporting a finding. It should say that the release check needs a local port and Chrome, and how to fix that.
Outside the sandbox the same release check passed.</li>
<li><code>lighthouse.max-potential-fid</code>, a diagnostic, appeared in 11 of 39 release builds that evening
across the five pages the agent built, while performance stayed at 100. A strict &quot;0 warnings&quot; rule then fails at random.
Diagnostics that do not move the score should be reported apart from findings, or measured twice before they
warn.</li>
</ul></article></main>
